    "media:imagetext": "7.\nularly as in my opinion the longer he stays in Nanking the more difti-\n•edi to be a very worth chap and I bet he is a true Christian, but he\nhad better go home and stay there for a while.\nPaxton came along so he and I went out ta the Uos.S. Panay in\na sampan and asked\nthe Captain to send me up to the Kung Wo. He was\nvery nice, Hank Jarrell was aboard, and in a few minutes I was going\nup on a Navy launch. Max had got the cabin 0.K. and was he glad to see\nme. We then sat here for an hour and took on what added up to over three\nthousand steerage passengers, and had to shove off to avoid being sw-\namped. There were two more big steam tenders on the way. We have a de-\ncided list and look like a Chinese ferry, but Max got a swell cabin for\ngovernment and dissentions but no one knows anything, and it is very\nexeiting. The Asst. Dir. Gen.of Posts is on board and so is Sun Fo's\nNo. One concubine:\nThat's a bad sign. When the number one concubines\nstart moving it means something. I guess Hankow will be just as bads\nprobably more crowded lor foreigners. No one can possibly foresee what\nis going to happen. The government may be able to pull itself together,\nit may all be rumor, or it may completely blow, in which case conditions\nin China for a while are going to be bad, but in any case I am afraid\nthere is nothing we can do for a while. Whwn I see Bix in Hankow I'1l\nknow more. I am anxious to see him.\nNow. Something\nJust East of Kiukiang\nWe got to Wuhu that night and there was such a mob waiting to\ncome et*- a board we couldn't doek, but had to anchor in midstream until\nthe Cus toms people could come aboard and clear us. About a hundred\ndid come out in sampans and scramble on board and when we started off\nagain the river was\nthick with sampans loaded with people coming out\nto us, but we escaped them. We have all we can carry. The Vice Minister\nof Finance is sleeping on a sofa in the lounge and hundreds are sleeping\non the deck, on the floor, and all over the place. There are several\nconcubines, numerous nurses and other women on board. We are running\nshort of food a little,\nnot serious at all. All the bread is gone, only\ncarrots and potatoes and beef. The water supply is getting low and we\ncan't get any more until we get to Hankow but that is only about twenty\nfour hours away so we shall make it safely.\nThe situation in China,\nthe interior, may become very acute,\nand\nI think everyone is preparing for a storm. However if my seven years in\nChina have taught me anything it is that China seems to weather all\nstorms and return to normalcy in a surprisingly short time. I am interest\nted to see Bix and find out what the latest is. You see they had a dir-\nector's and shareholder's meeting in Nanking while I was in Shanghai. I\nwas supposed to return to Nanking in time for it, but when the Japanese\nsurrounded Shanghai I couldn't get back in time..\nI didn't call on any\nof the Ministry officials when I came through Nanking for the very good\nreasons I didn't want to. Mr. Peck told me the results of the meeting\nwere negative, go Bix had informed him.\nMy s trenuous trips of the past ten days have done me a world\nof good. It has been a lot of hard living and hard work and I have to\nkeep my head \"cut in\" and that was what I needed.",
